BM-CF-01 the default-mailbox keys of reference.conf build a 1000 slot, 10 s mailbox -> capacity=1000 pushTimeOut=10000000000ns mailbox-type=org.apache.pekko.dispatch.UnboundedMailbox
BM-CF-02 push timeout values: units, negative, none, nanosecond precision, bad values -> 0 => capacity=1 pushTimeOut=0ns | 0s => capacity=1 pushTimeOut=0ns | 10s => capacity=1 pushTimeOut=10000000000ns | 250ms => capacity=1 pushTimeOut=250000000ns | 5 => capacity=1 pushTimeOut=5000000ns | 1ns => capacity=1 pushTimeOut=1ns | 2h => capacity=1 pushTimeOut=7200000000000ns | -1 => capacity=1 pushTimeOut=-1000000ns | -1ms => capacity=1 pushTimeOut=-1000000ns | -2s => capacity=1 pushTimeOut=-2000000000ns | infinite => ERR BadValue [config message elided] | abc => ERR BadValue [config message elided] | "10 parsecs" => ERR BadValue [config message elided] | "" => ERR BadValue [config message elided] | 1.5s => capacity=1 pushTimeOut=1500000000ns | null => ERR Null [config message elided]
BM-CF-03 capacity values: zero and one pass the constructor, negative and non-integers do not -> 0 => capacity=0 pushTimeOut=1000000000ns | 1 => capacity=1 pushTimeOut=1000000000ns | 1000 => capacity=1000 pushTimeOut=1000000000ns | 2147483647 => capacity=2147483647 pushTimeOut=1000000000ns | 2147483648 => ERR WrongType [config message elided] | -1 => ERR IllegalArgumentException [The capacity for BoundedMailbox can not be negative] | -2147483648 => ERR IllegalArgumentException [The capacity for BoundedMailbox can not be negative] | 1.5 => capacity=1 pushTimeOut=1000000000ns | "7" => capacity=7 pushTimeOut=1000000000ns | abc => ERR WrongType [config message elided] | null => ERR Null [config message elided]
BM-CF-04 a missing key is a ConfigException of that key -> noCapacity => ERR Missing [config message elided] | noTimeout => ERR Missing [config message elided] | none => ERR Missing [config message elided]
BM-CF-05 the case class constructor validates: negative capacity, null timeout; zero capacity is accepted -> negative=ERR IllegalArgumentException [The capacity for BoundedMailbox can not be negative] | nullTimeout=ERR IllegalArgumentException [The push time-out for BoundedMailbox can not be null] | zero=ok capacity=0 | max=ok capacity=2147483647
BM-CF-06 create(): capacity 0 is rejected by the queue it builds, not by the mailbox type -> cap=0 => ERR IllegalArgumentException [null] | cap=1 => org.apache.pekko.dispatch.BoundedMailbox$MessageQueue n=0 has=false remaining=1 | cap=3 => org.apache.pekko.dispatch.BoundedMailbox$MessageQueue n=0 has=false remaining=3 | cap=2147483647 => org.apache.pekko.dispatch.BoundedMailbox$MessageQueue n=0 has=false remaining=2147483647
BM-CF-07 the queue it makes is a BlockingQueue exposing its push timeout -> blocking=true bounded=true unboundedSemantics=false pushTimeOut=7000000ns n=0 has=false remaining=5
BM-MB-01 a configured BoundedMailbox with a non-zero timeout warns once per system, whatever the id -> a=BoundedMailbox(capacity=3 pushTimeOut=10000000000ns) warnings after a=[mailboxes|Configured potentially-blocking mailbox [a] configured with non-zero pushTimeOut (10000000000 nanoseconds), which can lead to blocking behavior when sending messages to this mailbox. Avoid this by setting `a.mailbox-push-timeout-time` to `0`.] | b=BoundedMailbox(capacity=4 pushTimeOut=1000000ns) warnings after b=[mailboxes|Configured potentially-blocking mailbox [a] configured with non-zero pushTimeOut (10000000000 nanoseconds), which can lead to blocking behavior when sending messages to this mailbox. Avoid this by setting `a.mailbox-push-timeout-time` to `0`.] | again a same=true
BM-MB-02 a zero timeout does not warn and does not use up the single warning -> afterZero=[] afterNegative=[] afterPositive=[mailboxes|Configured potentially-blocking mailbox [p] configured with non-zero pushTimeOut (5000000000 nanoseconds), which can lead to blocking behavior when sending messages to this mailbox. Avoid this by setting `p.mailbox-push-timeout-time` to `0`.]
BM-MB-03 bounded-capacity:N builds BoundedMailbox(N, 0) and never warns -> bounded-capacity:5 => BoundedMailbox(capacity=5 pushTimeOut=0ns) | bounded-capacity:1 => BoundedMailbox(capacity=1 pushTimeOut=0ns) | bounded-capacity:0 => BoundedMailbox(capacity=0 pushTimeOut=0ns) | bounded-capacity:2147483647 => BoundedMailbox(capacity=2147483647 pushTimeOut=0ns) | bounded-capacity:-1 => ERR IllegalArgumentException [The capacity for BoundedMailbox can not be negative] | bounded-capacity: => ERR ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException [Index 1 out of bounds for length 1] | bounded-capacity:x => ERR NumberFormatException [For input string: "x"] | bounded-capacity:5:6 => BoundedMailbox(capacity=5 pushTimeOut=0ns) | bounded-capacity: 7 => ERR NumberFormatException [For input string: " 7"] | warnings=[]
BM-MB-04 lookup errors: unknown id, the literal id bounded, a class that is not a mailbox, a negative capacity in config -> nope => ERR ConfigurationException [Mailbox Type [nope] not configured] | bounded => ERR Missing [config message elided] | bad-class => ERR IllegalArgumentException [Cannot instantiate MailboxType [java.lang.String], defined in [bad-class], make sure it has a public constructor with [org.apache.pekko.actor.ActorSystem.Settings, com.typesafe.config.Config] parameters] cause=ERR ClassCastException [interface org.apache.pekko.dispatch.MailboxType is not assignable from class java.lang.String] | neg => ERR IllegalArgumentException [Cannot instantiate MailboxType [org.apache.pekko.dispatch.BoundedMailbox], defined in [neg], make sure it has a public constructor with [org.apache.pekko.actor.ActorSystem.Settings, com.typesafe.config.Config] parameters] cause=ERR IllegalArgumentException [The capacity for BoundedMailbox can not be negative] | empty => ERR ConfigurationException [The setting mailbox-type, defined in [empty] is empty]
BM-MB-05 an actor that requires BoundedMessageQueueSemantics gets the configured BoundedMailbox (1000, 10 s) -> queue=org.apache.pekko.dispatch.BoundedMailbox$MessageQueue n=0 has=false remaining=1000 | lookupByQueueType=BoundedMailbox(capacity=1000 pushTimeOut=10000000000ns)
BM-MB-06 BoundedMailbox produces a BoundedMailbox.MessageQueue, which fulfils BoundedMessageQueueSemantics and not the unbounded one -> produced=org.apache.pekko.dispatch.BoundedMailbox$MessageQueue bounded=true unbounded=false blocking=true
BM-QU-01 timeout 0: a full queue refuses at once, the refused go to dead letters in order, the queue keeps the first -> new: n=0 has=false remaining=3 | a: n=1 has=true remaining=2 | b: n=2 has=true remaining=1 | c: n=3 has=true remaining=0 | d: n=3 has=true remaining=0 | e: n=3 has=true remaining=0 | dead=[d<-snd->recv, e<-snd->recv] | drain=[a, b, c] n=0 has=false remaining=3
BM-QU-02 dequeue frees a slot: the next enqueue is accepted and the order is still first in first out -> first=a rest=[b, d] dead=[c<-recv->recv, e<-recv->recv] n=0 has=false remaining=2
BM-QU-03 capacity 1: one accepted, the rest refused -> n=1 has=true remaining=0 drain=[a] dead=[b<-recv->recv, c<-recv->recv]
BM-QU-04 the dead letter carries the message, the envelope's sender and the receiver; a DeadLetter message is not dropped -> dead=[refused<-snd->recv, noSender<-deadLetters->recv, DeadLetter(inner)<-snd->recv] queue=[kept]
BM-QU-05 timeout > 0: the producer parks for at least the timeout, then the message is a dead letter -> waitedAtLeastTimeout=true waitedUnderTenSeconds=true dead=[late<-recv->recv] queue=[kept]
BM-QU-06 timeout > 0: a dequeue during the wait lets the parked producer through, nothing is dead-lettered -> parked: state=TIMED_WAITING n=1 has=true remaining=0 | first=a producerAlive=false rest=[b] dead=[]
BM-QU-07 timeout < 0: the producer waits without a deadline until space appears -> parked: state=WAITING n=1 has=true remaining=0 | first=a producerAlive=false rest=[b] dead=[]
BM-QU-08 an interrupted blocked producer fails with InterruptedException: nothing queued, nothing dead-lettered -> timeout=positive: ERR InterruptedException [null] n=1 has=true remaining=0 queue=[a] | timeout=negative: ERR InterruptedException [null] n=1 has=true remaining=0 queue=[a] | dead=[]
BM-QU-09 cleanUp moves a full queue to the target in order and frees every slot -> source n=1 has=true remaining=2 | target n=3 order=[a, b, c] | sourceAfterNewEnqueue=[after]
BM-QU-10 a producer parked on a full queue is let through by cleanUp and its message stays in the cleaned queue or goes to the target, never lost -> producerDone=true movedPlusStranded=3 dead=[] firstTwoMovedInOrder=[a, b]
BM-QC-01 four producers, timeout 0, nobody dequeues: exactly capacity accepted, every other message dead-lettered -> accepted=10 dead=190 acceptedOrdered=true deadOrdered=true everyMessageAccountedOnce=true
BM-QC-02 four producers and one consumer, long timeout: nothing lost, per-producer order, the queue never above capacity -> received=200 distinct=200 perProducerOrdered=true neverAboveCapacity=true dead=0 n=0 has=false remaining=5
BM-AC-01 an actor with a full bounded mailbox: the first `capacity` messages are processed, the rest are dead letters in order -> inTurn: queued=3 dead=[m4<-snd->w, m5<-snd->w, m6<-snd->w] | afterRelease: log=[block, m1, m2, m3] queued=0 dead=[m4<-snd->w, m5<-snd->w, m6<-snd->w]
BM-AC-02 the message being processed does not occupy a slot: capacity messages queue behind it -> inTurn before: queued=0 | after two sends: queued=1 dead=[m2<-deadLetters->w] | log=[block, m1]
BM-AC-03 backpressure: with a long timeout the sender thread parks while the mailbox is full and everything arrives in order -> senderState=TIMED_WAITING queued=2 | received=21 inOrder=true dead=[] senderDone=true
BM-AC-04 four producers, timeout 0, consumer held: exactly capacity accepted, per-producer order, the rest dead -> inTurn: queued=5 dead=95 | received=5 perProducerOrdered=true dead=95 deadOrdered=true everyMessageAccountedOnce=true
BM-AC-05 four producers, long timeout, consumer running: nothing lost, per-producer order, mailbox never above capacity, one turn at a time -> received=200 distinct=200 perProducerOrdered=true mailboxNeverAboveCapacity=true maxInFlight=1 dead=0
BM-AC-06 system messages are not bounded: a full mailbox still takes Suspend/Resume and they run before the queued user messages -> full: queued=2 | systemMessagesSent: queued=2 hasSystemMessages=true dead=[] | afterTurn: suspendCount=1 queued=2 log=[block] | afterResume: log=[block, m1, m2] suspendCount=0
BM-AC-07 a suspended actor with a full mailbox stays unscheduled, refuses further user messages, and resumes with the queued ones -> suspended: queued=2 scheduled=false dead=[m3<-deadLetters->w, m4<-deadLetters->w] log=[] | afterResume: log=[m1, m2, m5] dead=[m3<-deadLetters->w, m4<-deadLetters->w]
BM-AC-08 a sender parked on a full mailbox is a suspended actor's problem too: with a timeout it ends in a dead letter -> waitedAtLeastTimeout=true dead=[m2<-deadLetters->w] queued=1
BM-AC-09 stop while the mailbox is full: Terminate overtakes the queued user messages, which become dead letters in order -> log=[block] dead=[m4<-deadLetters->w, m1<-deadLetters->w, m2<-deadLetters->w, m3<-deadLetters->w] oldMailbox: closed=true queued=0
BM-AC-10 a stopped actor does not block its senders, whatever the timeout: the closed mailbox is the dead-letter mailbox -> senderDone=true dead=[x<-deadLetters->w, y<-deadLetters->w]
BM-AC-11 a sender parked on a full mailbox when the actor stops: every message is dead-lettered or stranded in the old queue, none is processed -> senderDone=true processed=[block] deadPlusStranded=3 firstTwoDeadInOrder=[m1, m2]
BM-AC-12 a sender on the actor's own single-thread pool starves it: the push timeout is what ends the wait -> senderTurnWaitedAtLeastTimeout=true received=[first] dead=[second<-sender->target]
BM-AC-13 Props.withMailbox with capacity 0: accepted by the lookup, the actor's cell cannot be made -> lookup=BoundedMailbox(capacity=0 pushTimeOut=0ns) | actorOf returned started=0 terminated=true
BM-AC-14 Props.withMailbox with a negative capacity in config: the lookup fails when the actor is created -> actorOf threw ERR ConfigurationException [configuration problem while creating [pekko://bm/user/w] with dispatcher [t-disp] and mailbox [bm]] cause=ERR IllegalArgumentException [Cannot instantiate MailboxType [org.apache.pekko.dispatch.BoundedMailbox], defined in [bm], make sure it has a public constructor with [org.apache.pekko.actor.ActorSystem.Settings, com.typesafe.config.Config] parameters]
BM-AC-15 the dispatcher's own mailbox-type key selects the bounded mailbox for its actors -> queue=org.apache.pekko.dispatch.BoundedMailbox$MessageQueue remaining=2
BM-AC-16 a sender interrupted while parked on a full mailbox: ActorCell swallows the InterruptedException, publishes an Error and restores the interrupt flag; the message is neither queued nor dead-lettered -> sender: returned interruptedFlag=true errors=[InterruptedException|interrupted during message send] queued=1 | log=[block, m1] dead=[]
BM-UC-01 messages held by the UnstartedCell are delivered into the bounded mailbox when it starts: the first `capacity` are kept, the overflow is dead-lettered in order -> log=[m1, m2] dead=[m3<-spawner->c, m4<-spawner->c]
BM-UC-02 the drain runs on the thread of the actor that attaches the child: with a one-thread pool nobody consumes meanwhile, so each overflowing message is dead-lettered once its push timeout expires -> log=[m1] dead=[m2<-spawner->c, m3<-spawner->c]
BM-UC-03 a sender that meets the UnstartedCell while it drains into a full bounded mailbox waits unstarted-push-timeout for the lock, then its message is a dead letter with a warning -> senderWaitedAtLeastLockTimeout=true senderWaitedLessThanPushTimeout=true log=[m1] dead=[late<-deadLetters->c, m2<-spawner->c] lockWarnings=[pekko://bm/user/spawner/c|dropping message of typeclass java.lang.String due to lock timeout]
